\f0\fs24 \cf2 The ICKS system integrated design specification.\
\
The ICKS system is a system that can be defined through the geometric structural properties for which a predicated outcome is produces, a pressure release vessel optimized for state of matter phase change generational warfare technology.\
\
The mechanics are as follows:\
\
Induction heat to produce internal temperature increasing activity internally\
Medium of ammunition is P1S and structural enhancers in water soluble solution\
External temperature buffer for emergence of hardened shell, being liquid nitrogen.\
All of this is encapsulated into a reusable containment area with a shoot, where the pressure building vessel result is emitted.\
\
Internally there is a metal apparatus for which can be heated up via induction heating using EMF.\
\
The result is a shell ammunition for which the trigger is the internal heating meeting with the external cooling at extremes which causes a violent triggering pressure building vessel reaction that acts upon a threshold.\
\
This is a modular system, whereby the induction heating chamber is the ammunition chamber and the reaction is triggered by the heat induction itself. The ammunition priming itself can be done before the ammunition enters the chamber, but the ammunition must be created and chambered within a set expiry period otherwise the ammunition will expire. Therefore the utilization of the ICKS system and the loading of the ICKS system will be done during deployment. The induction heating chamber is a reusable mechanism. The priming of the ammunition and the ammunition itself, the clearing of the chamber and reloading process, is where the automated process focus is.\
\
So let\'92s work based on the creation of the ammunition all the way to deployment so we can understand practically how to utilize the ICKS system.\
\
We begin with what amounts to a cannonball metal induced material. The size of this is not set, it can be set based on pre-set specifications and utility. We then have the P1S substance that we encapsulate around the cannonball to form a frozen outer shell encasing the cannonball where the cannonball is at the center. The process for the accumulation of frozen P1S around the cannonball is important but the outer shell for which the real interaction occurs is likely not composed through this process. What emerges is a cannonball encased in P1S ice where the cannonball is at the center. Towards the end of the process, a deliberate effort for ensuring the structure of the shell encasing is as structurally reinforced as possible to create the strongest vessel for pressure build up and release. There is a designated distance from the cannonball where between cannonball and the barrier, the ice will melt so the structure of that ice may not be as important. However the ice barrier that defines the outer shell must be up to certain threshold. Once this is established, we have the initial shell this is layer 1 of ammunition creation and preservation. At this state, the preservation cost of storing the ammunition takes place, there is a cost to hold this state.\
\
From this state we have further preparation, we can stop layer 1 process at a critical threshold where the deliberate creation of the structurally reinforced layer is composed deliberately where it is then encased in liquid nitrogen so that the external thermal buffer is sustaining the outer shell, it is likely the formation of this layering will be enforced by liquid nitrogen cooling to retain structural integrity for when the ammunition is deployed. This is where the cost of storage compounds with cost of liquid nitrogen and structural maintenance of the outer shell. Ammunition at this point of the layering carry increased cost basis.\
\
From this point there is the 3rd layer, loading into the chamber. When this is loaded into the chamber, it will still while in chamber be encased with liquid nitrogen and is still encased throughout entire process until release event. This ensures the outermost layer still has the thermodynamic force of cooling continuously from outside to reinforce the thermodynamic cooling effect is sustained to maximize the matter state transition tension. While it is being loaded into chamber, the induction heating can begin to prepare the internal state of the ammunition. This is so that the heating is not done so that it is metal on ice, which is inefficient. When the induction heating is applied at maximum capacity, the cannonball should be encased in water. Therefore there is a small pocket internally where it is cannonball -> water -> internal ice -> outer reinforced shell. This is a time sensitive portion for which deployment of the ammunition must be done in a set calculable amount of time otherwise the ammunition expires.\
\
I envision the chamber to be very tight fit with the ammunition with a layer of liquid nitrogen up to a shoot for which the pressure will escape from in a concentrated flow. The outer chamber must be sufficiently reinforced in strength to handle the triggering as well as house the heat induction apparatus. Thus the walls of the chamber must never interact with the induction heating and must sustain constant rounds of firing relativistically to cost and utility. Upon loading into chamber, 3rd layer is utilized with heating up center for initial prep. Once it is in final chamber the induction heating is on full blast until critical point of pressure release. \
\
Upon release, the remaining contents of chamber are swapped out, any residual contents disposed of immediately with full power of what is available as the new round is reloaded through the assembly process and repeats.\
\
Additionally, there is an option to add water soluble additive agents to the water that composes P1S which can be utilized to enhance the properties of P1S for the use case of the ICKS system, therefore additional ingredients can be explored for which to add to enhance the outcome of the ICKS system.\
\
The merit of the ICKS system does not come from gradual build up, it works off of creating a thermodynamic temporal singularity event. The superheating of metallic inner core with the coupling of the liquid nitrogen external cooling factor over a very short temporal window structurally requires the outcome we are trying to achieve. This is not gradual built up and release, this is \'93Can pressure rise faster than the shell can physically disassemble?\'94. It is likely that the cannonball metallic structural inner core is not sufficient and emphasis on surface area for thermodynamic heating efficiency in core during heat induction, this points to a mesh. Therefore layer 1 may just be the P1S encapsulating the inner mesh! The real emphasis is thermal transfer speed!\
\
